examples.question_text:
A triangular prism has length $6$ cm. The red dashed outline shows a slice parallel to its triangular end faces. Sketch and name the cross-section.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[3,0,0],"C":[1.5,2,0],"D":[0,0,3],"E":[3,0,3],"F":[1.5,2,3]},"faces":[["A","B","C"],["D","F","E"],["A","D","E","B"],["B","E","F","C"],["C","F","D","A"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.3],"toward":[0.5,0.3,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,scale=0.8]
\definecolor{sectionRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(1.5,2)--cycle;
\draw (3,0)--(4.5,0.9)--(3,2.9)--(1.5,2);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(1.5,0.9)--(4.5,0.9);
\draw[dashed] (1.5,0.9)--(3,2.9);
\draw[sectionRed,dashed,thick] (0.75,0.45)--(3.75,0.45)--(2.25,2.45)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The slice is parallel to the triangular bases, so it has the same shape and size as either base. The cross-section is a triangle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,scale=0.8]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(1.5,2)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both cross-sections are squares. The square nearer the apex is smaller.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (2,2);
\draw (3.5,0.5) rectangle (4.5,1.5);
\path (1,-0.4) node {Lower slice};
\path (4,-0.4) node {Higher slice};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The slices have the same shape but different sizes, so the pyramid does not have a uniform cross-section.

Solid $A$: every slice parallel to a circular end face is an identical circle. It has a uniform cross-section, but it is not a prism because a circle is not a polygon.

Solid $B$: every slice parallel to a pentagonal end face is an identical pentagon. It has a uniform cross-section and is a prism because its end faces are polygons joined by rectangular sides.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont]
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\draw (3,1)--(3.951,0.309)--(3.588,-0.809)--(2.412,-0.809)--(2.049,0.309)--cycle;
\path (0,-1.4) node {$A$: circle};
\path (3,-1.4) node {$B$: pentagon};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
A triangular prism is resting on a rectangular face. The perpendicular distance between its triangular end faces is $8$ cm. Identify its bases and its height, then name its uniform cross-section.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[3,0,0],"C":[1.5,2,0],"D":[0,0,3],"E":[3,0,3],"F":[1.5,2,3]},"faces":[["A","B","C"],["D","F","E"],["A","D","E","B"],["B","E","F","C"],["C","F","D","A"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.3],"toward":[0.5,0.3,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,scale=0.8]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(1.5,2)--cycle;
\draw (3,0)--(4.5,0.9)--(3,2.9)--(1.5,2);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(1.5,0.9)--(4.5,0.9);
\draw[dashed] (1.5,0.9)--(3,2.9);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The two triangular end faces are the bases. The prism's height is the perpendicular distance between these faces, so $h=8$ cm, measured along the prism's length.

Slices parallel to the triangular end faces are identical triangles, so its uniform cross-section is a triangle.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","model":{"version":1,"kind":"polyhedron","vertices":{"A":[0,0,0],"B":[3,0,0],"C":[1.5,2,0],"D":[0,0,3],"E":[3,0,3],"F":[1.5,2,3]},"faces":[["A","B","C"],["D","F","E"],["A","D","E","B"],["B","E","F","C"],["C","F","D","A"]],"view":{"right":[1,0,0.5],"up":[0,1,0.3],"toward":[0.5,0.3,-1]}}}
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,scale=0.8,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(1.5,2)--cycle;
\draw (3,0)--(4.5,0.9)--(3,2.9)--(1.5,2);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(1.5,0.9)--(4.5,0.9);
\draw[dashed] (1.5,0.9)--(3,2.9);
\draw[<->] (3.2,-0.35)--(4.7,0.55);
\path (4.25,-0.15) node[right] {$h=8$ cm};
\path (1.5,0.65) node {Base};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

